From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] vmdk: an extent ending at the sought offset is below it, not above

`VmdkExtent::disk_range` is a half-open `Range`, so an extent covers
`[start, end)`. `get_extent_at` searches for the extent covering `offset`
with a comparator that reports `Less` only when `end < offset`. When
`offset == end`, the extent lies entirely BELOW the target -- but the
comparator reports `Greater`, telling the search to look further left.

That breaks `binary_search_by`'s contract, and the search can then walk
away from the correct extent and return `Err`. `get_extent_at` maps that
to `None`, `get_mapping` turns it into `ShallowMapping::Eof`, and
`FormatAccess::readv` fills the buffer with zeros and returns SUCCESS.
The caller is handed zeroed data with no error of any kind.

Any offset that coincides with an extent boundary is exposed, which for
a partitioned flat VMDK is every partition start. Whether a given one
survives depends on which extents the search happens to probe first, so
the failure looks arbitrary: with an 18-extent disk, partitions 1-3 are
found and 4 onwards are not.

Measured through libkrun on Windows: a GPT-partitioned flat VMDK with
one EROFS partition per container image layer, where the guest mounted
partitions 1, 2 and 3 and then reported

    erofs: (device vdc4): erofs_read_superblock: cannot find valid erofs
    superblock

with no I/O error anywhere, because the zeros were delivered as a
successful read. Reproduced against this comparator and the real
`binary_search_by` for two unrelated images, at 4 MiB and at 2.3 GB.
---
 src/vmdk/mod.rs | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/vmdk/mod.rs
+++ b/src/vmdk/mod.rs
@@ -514,7 +514,11 @@ impl<S: Storage + 'static, F: WrappedFormat<S> + 'static> Vmdk<S, F> {
             .binary_search_by(|extent| {
                 if extent.disk_range.contains(&offset) {
                     cmp::Ordering::Equal
-                } else if extent.disk_range.end < offset {
+                } else if extent.disk_range.end <= offset {
+                    // `disk_range` is half-open, so an extent whose end EQUALS `offset` does
+                    // not contain it and lies below it. Reporting `Greater` here sends the
+                    // search the wrong way, it returns `Err`, and that becomes a zero-filled
+                    // read reported as success.
                     cmp::Ordering::Less
                 } else {
                     cmp::Ordering::Greater
